## Decoding the question
Two parts. **"Root causes"** asks for the **deep** conditions that make GBV common and gendered — not just triggers such as a quarrel. **"How do these factors intersect and perpetuate"** is the analytical core: show how causes **reinforce one another**, how they combine with women's **identities** (intersectionality), and how they **sustain** violence over time. A cycle figure is ideal.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Gender-based violence** is harm directed at a person because of their gender, rooted in **unequal power** between women and men; the UN and WHO identify **gender inequality** and **harmful social norms** as its root causes everywhere.

#### ii) The argument
In Pakistan, the root of GBV is a **patriarchal social order** that places family honour in women's bodies and authority in men's hands. Two further causes — women's **economic dependence** and the near-total **impunity** of perpetrators — allow that root to keep producing violence, while customs, ideas and crises add to it. These causes do not act separately: they **intersect**, reinforce one another and fall hardest on women who are poor, rural or from minorities, creating a self-perpetuating **cycle**.

### 2. Root causes and triggers
A **root cause** explains why violence is common and directed at women across society; a **trigger** explains why it erupts in a particular case — a dowry dispute, a job loss, drug use, a daughter's choice of husband. **Lori Heise**'s **ecological model** (==1998==) organises causes at four levels — **individual, relationship, community and society** — and shows that violence results from their interaction. Feminist theory adds that violence is a means of **power and control**.

### 3. Root cause 1: patriarchy, honour and guardianship

#### i) Male authority
Many Pakistanis believe a husband may "discipline" his wife; in the **PDHS 2017–18**, large shares of women and men said wife-beating was justified in some circumstances.

#### ii) Honour
A family's *izzat* is believed to depend on its women's chastity and obedience, so any perceived breach — marrying by choice, seeking divorce, appearing on social media — can be punished, even by death. At least **405** honour killings were recorded in ==2024== (HRCP).

#### iii) Son preference and control of marriage
Daughters are seen as "*paraya dhan*"; forced marriage and *watta satta* deny women choice, although the Supreme Court held in ***Saima Waheed*** (==2004==) that an adult woman may marry without a guardian's consent.

### 4. Root cause 2: economic dependence and poverty
Female labour-force participation is only ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25), and women rarely own land or receive their **inheritance**. A woman without income or property cannot easily leave an abusive home. **Poverty** and unemployment add stress, dowry demands and conflict over money — triggers that, as **resource theory** (Goode, ==1971==) predicts, raise the risk of violence when men cannot assert authority in other ways.

### 5. Root cause 3: impunity
The most powerful sustaining cause is the near-certainty that perpetrators will not be punished. In ==2024==, the **Sustainable Social Development Organization** found conviction rates of about **0.5%** for rape and honour killing, **1.3%** for domestic violence and **0.1%** for kidnapping; in Punjab, **225** honour killings produced just **two** convictions. Police refuse or delay FIRs, investigations lack forensic evidence, families pressure victims to withdraw, and trials take years. Until ==2016==, honour killers could be **pardoned** by their own relatives — and even after reform, the acquittal of **Qandeel Baloch**'s brother in ==2022== showed how settlements still free killers.

### 6. Root cause 4: customs and parallel justice
**Jirgas** and **panchayats** of male elders, outside the law, order ***vani*** and ***swara*** (girls given in settlement), declare women *kari* and approve their killing, and in the **Mukhtaran Mai** case (==2002==) ordered a gang rape as "punishment". In ==2025==, a couple were shot dead in **Degari**, Balochistan, allegedly on a tribal chief's orders. The **Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act 2011** criminalised these customs, but parallel justice persists where state courts are distant, slow and costly.

### 7. Root cause 5: ideas — literacy, religion and media
Female literacy is about ==53%==, and many women do not know their rights. **Social learning theory** shows that boys who see their fathers beat their mothers learn that violence is acceptable. Some clerics and elders **misuse religion** to justify beating, child marriage or honour killing, though mainstream scholars reject honour killing as murder. Some television dramas romanticise controlling husbands, and social media spreads harassment.

### 8. Root cause 6: crises
Militancy brought attacks on girls' schools and on **Malala Yousafzai** (==2012==); displacement exposes women to exploitation; the ==2022== floods disrupted protection systems and raised the risk of early marriage. Research worldwide shows violence against women rises during crises, including the **COVID-19** lockdowns of ==2020==.

::: fig cycle | How the root causes intersect and perpetuate violence against women in Pakistan.
@centre The cycle of violence perpetuated
Patriarchal norms justify male control and "honour"
Girls kept from school, work and property
Economic dependence traps women in violent homes
Shame, fear and family pressure silence victims
Police inaction, pardons and jirgas give impunity
Impunity teaches that violence works — norms are confirmed
:::

### 9. Intersection 1: how the causes reinforce one another

#### i) Norms and economy
Honour norms keep women at home and out of paid work; economic dependence then keeps them silent.

#### ii) Economy and law
Poor women cannot afford lawyers or travel to courts, while abusers with money and connections can buy settlements.

#### iii) Law and custom
Where courts fail, *jirgas* fill the gap — and their judgements reflect patriarchal norms.

#### iv) Ideas and institutions
Police officers who share the belief that "family matters" are private refuse to act, turning cultural attitudes into institutional impunity.

The result is a **self-reinforcing cycle**: norms justify control, control produces violence, impunity confirms that violence works, and the norms are strengthened.

### 10. Intersection 2: identities
Using **intersectionality** (**Kimberlé Crenshaw**, ==1989==, ==1991==), the causes fall hardest on women who face several disadvantages at once:
- **rural and poor women**, far from police and courts and dependent on landlords and *jirgas*;
- **Hindu and Christian girls**, reported each year to be abducted, forcibly converted and married, especially in Sindh and Punjab;
- **women with disabilities**, more vulnerable to abuse and less able to report;
- **domestic workers**, often young girls, abused in employers' homes;
- ***khawaja siras*** (transgender persons), facing violence despite the **Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2018**.

For these women, gender combines with class, religion, disability or gender identity, so violence is more likely and justice less reachable.

### 11. Intersection 3: across the ecological levels

::: table | Root causes at the four levels of the ecological model.
@widths 3,11
Level | Causes in Pakistan
Society | Patriarchal norms and honour codes; impunity; misused religion; crises
Community | Jirgas and panchayats; poverty and unemployment; isolation of women
Relationship | Husband's control of money; joint-family pressure; dowry disputes; forced marriage
Individual | Witnessing violence in childhood; low education; drug use; belief in male authority
:::

A husband who saw his father beat his mother (individual), who controls the household's money (relationship), in a village where *jirgas* settle disputes (community), in a society that tolerates "disciplining" wives and rarely punishes violence (society), is far more likely to be violent — and far less likely to face consequences.

### 12. Breaking the cycle and conclusion

#### i) Breaking the cycle
Because the causes reinforce one another, breaking any link weakens the whole: **ending impunity** through compulsory FIRs, forensic evidence, special courts and non-compoundable offences; **economic independence** through jobs, inheritance and cash transfers to women; and **changing norms** through schools, religious scholars, the media and work with men and boys.

#### ii) Conclusion
The root cause of gender-based violence in Pakistan is a patriarchal order that ties honour to women's bodies and authority to men. Economic dependence, impunity, parallel justice, harmful ideas and crises intersect with it — and with women's class, religion and disability — to create a cycle that perpetuates violence across generations. Only a strategy that attacks **norms, dependence and impunity together** can break it.

## Why this answer scores
- Distinguishes **root causes** from **triggers** and uses the **ecological model**.
- Gives **six root causes** with current Pakistani evidence (PDHS, SSDO, HRCP, cases).
- Answers "**intersect**" at three levels — causes, identities and ecological levels.
- A **cycle figure** shows how violence is perpetuated.
